Refuse the object read path on a store the page service has run on.

With the page service on the crate elides the eager put per page write,
because the layers answer reads and writing an 8 KB object per page write
is the thing the storage layer exists to stop doing. The objects under
pg/ then hold whatever they held when that session opened, while the
checkpoints keep advancing on the layers above them.

Start the same store with the service off and the read path goes back to
those objects. Recovery begins at a redo location far past the pages it
is applying records to, and a heap record lands on a page whose line
pointers it does not describe: three postmasters in a row died with
PANIC: invalid lp, and then the node gave up (zou #462). A clean
shutdown makes it quieter and no better, since recovery has nothing to
replay and the node comes up serving a catalog from before the flag day,
where a table created after it does not exist.

The crate marks the point in the manifest the first time a store opens
with the service on and decides whether a capture has been taken since,
which is what makes it a refusal rather than a state to discover. This
side only has to ask before recovery instead of during it, so the
postmaster puts the question at the one place it already asks whether
the service is on at all, and answers a yes with a FATAL that names both
LSNs and says what a comparison of the two paths needs instead.

A store nobody can read at all answers neither yes nor no and is left
alone: the open right after this has the real error for it.

--- a/src/backend/access/transam/zou_wal.c
+++ b/src/backend/access/transam/zou_wal.c
@@ -56,6 +56,7 @@
 extern int	zou_pagesvc_start(const char *target, int data_checksums);
 extern int	zou_pagesvc_stop(void);
 extern int	zou_pageserve_on(void);
+extern int	zou_pages_left_behind(uint64 *elided, uint64 *captured);
 
 #define ZOU_ERR_LEASE_HELD (-5)
 #define ZOU_ERR_THROTTLED (-7)
@@ -130,6 +131,42 @@
 		ZouWalShmem->pusher_latch = NULL;
 		ZouWalShmem->pusher_pid = 0;
 	}
+}
+
+/*
+ * Refuse to start on the object read path over a store whose page
+ * objects a page service session has left behind.
+ *
+ * With the service on the eager put per page write is elided, because
+ * the layers answer reads and writing an 8 KB object per page write is
+ * the thing the storage layer exists to stop doing. The objects under
+ * pg/ then hold whatever they held when that session opened, while the
+ * checkpoints keep advancing on top of the layers. Reading them again
+ * with the service off puts recovery at a redo location far past the
+ * pages it is applying records to, and the first heap record to land on
+ * a page whose line pointers it does not describe brings the postmaster
+ * down with "invalid lp" (zou #462).
+ *
+ * The crate carries the mark in the manifest and decides, this only has
+ * to say so before recovery rather than during it. A store nobody can
+ * read answers neither yes nor no and is left to the open below, which
+ * has the real error for it.
+ */
+static void
+zou_check_pages_are_ours(void)
+{
+	uint64		elided = 0;
+	uint64		captured = 0;
+
+	if (zou_pages_left_behind(&elided, &captured) != 1)
+		return;
+
+	ereport(FATAL,
+			(errmsg("zou store has no page objects past %X/%X to read",
+					LSN_FORMAT_ARGS(elided)),
+			 errdetail("The store ran with the page service on from %X/%X, which stops writing a page object per page write, and it has captured through %X/%X since. Starting with the service off would replay wal onto pages that stopped moving at the earlier point.",
+					   LSN_FORMAT_ARGS(elided), LSN_FORMAT_ARGS(captured)),
+			 errhint("Start this store with the page service on. Comparing the two read paths needs a store that has only ever run with it off.")));
 }
 
 void
@@ -178,6 +215,7 @@
 	switch (zou_pageserve_on())
 	{
 		case 0:
+			zou_check_pages_are_ours();
 			return;
 		case 1:
 			break;
